The main objective of the present paper is to propose a methodology for the reformulation of vortex wake models in state space form, suitable for direct coupling with time-marching simulations, and for linearized stability and response analyses. This methodology is based on the use of the method of lines, which is a technique for the numerical solution of PDE in which only the dependency on space is discretized in finite difference form (Ref. 19), with the result that the PDE are converted into a system of coupled ODE. In this paper, the MOL will be applied to the solution of the simplified vortex wake equation, Eq. (2). The specific objectives of the paper are: 1) to summarize the main features of the method of lines; 2) to discuss the main issues concerning the space discretization of Eq. (2), and the subsequent time-marching solution;
3) to extract a linearized state-space model of the vortex wake in first-order form, suitable for eigenanalysis; and 4) to present results showing the main numerical characteristics of the MOL-based technique, as a function of a variety of discretization and solution parameters. It is important to point out that the nonlinear term on the right-hand side of the complete governing equation for the wake, Eq. (1) contains many key aspects of the problem, both from the physical and the computational point of view. It is in the correct formulation and treatment of this term that many of the challenges of accurate free vortex wake analyses lie. Therefore, the results presented in this paper, which are limited to the simplified version of Eq. (2), should be considered as just a first step toward the objective of deriving accurate free wake models in state-space form using the method of lines. It should also be pointed out that the objective of this study is not to derive a more accurate free wake model, but simply to recast an existing model into a mathematical form more suitable for stability analyses and control system design. As far as this study is concerned, the main criterion to judge the accuracy of the reformulated wake is how closely it matches the results of the original wake. Also, a rigid wake is used in the present paper simply as a convenient test case for which an analytical solution is available. No claims are made concerning its suitability to model any specific flight condition. For these reasons, the results will not contain any comparisons with experimental data or with more sophisticated wake models. Finally, because the MOL can be applied to any PDE, it can provide, at least in principle, a systematic solution to the problem of generating CFD models in state-space form, and of coupling them to helicopter simulation models already composed of systems of ODE.
